WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.080
Going out and talking to random voters is
a really great thing to do during elections.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:13.119
Truly an MSNBC did that yesterday and hilariously
ran into a voter who said it is your coverage

00:00:13.119 --> 00:00:14.280
of Bernie Sanders.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:21.270
MSNBC is coverage of Bernie Sanders that made
her decide to vote for Bernie Sanders.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.580
This is super interesting.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.130
Ari Melbourne was in Manchester, New Hampshire
yesterday.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.420
The day of the primary goes up to a voter.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.800
She says, it was you that made me vote for
Bernie.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.030
Let's take a look at the video.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.540
What about over here?

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.540
Who did you vote for?

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.720
I voted for Bernie.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.280
Um, although there are a number of candidates
that I really like, and so I felt like I,

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.329
I, when I finished voting, I was still undecided,
um, including Elizabeth, including Michael

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:48.329
Bennett.

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.079
Now that's sort of a paradox to, to decide
and then feel undecided.

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:53.079
So you're a complex person.

00:00:53.079 --> 00:01:00.440
Well, yes, but I want to say the reason I
went for Bernie is on, because of MSNBC and

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:11.200
gone, I think it is completely cynical to
say that he's lost 50% of his vote on from

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.100
the last time when there were two candidates.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.470
Now there are multiple wonderful candidates
who would be great presidents and people that

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:23.510
we could, I think that we can unify and get
behind, but the, the, the kind of the stock

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:31.090
Bernie CISM cynicism that I heard from a number
of people, I watch MSNBC constantly.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.930
So I heard that from a number of commentators.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.150
And so that just, it made me angry enough.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.950
I said, okay, Bernie's got my vote.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:37.950
This is such a

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:38.950
interesting point.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.730
What you're saying is, and we take criticism
cause we're journalists, right?

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.049
We gotta be open minded.

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:46.780
You're saying that hearing from people, whether
it's gas contributors, the conversations you've

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:52.210
heard that you felt were designed to tear
down a Senator Sanders or just quote unquote

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.400
stop him actually endeared him to you.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.400
Absolutely.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.509
Now you'll notice how Ari Melbourne slips
in.

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:04.140
You're saying you've heard from guests and
contributors these things.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.140
Well.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.409
What about hosts are in Melbourne sort of
slipping in.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:09.920
You're not talking about MSNBC hosts.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.930
Obviously you're talking about guests who
are not affiliated with us.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:18.180
You're talking about contributors who of course
are actually affiliated with MSNBC.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.140
Uh, but in reality, it's also been the hosts
on MSNBC.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.510
Now the word cynicism was mentioned by the
voter.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:32.620
Cynicism is defined as an inclination to believe
that people are motivated purely by self-interest.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.670
Skepticism sometimes has overlap with cynicism.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:41.390
What's interesting about this is that on the
one hand, one of the biggest contributors

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:48.090
to the Republican national committee has been
the parent company of MSNBC, Comcast, the

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.930
big cable and internet company provider.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:57.920
Check out some of these donations from last
year, 45,000 bucks to the RNC, another 45,000

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.030
bucks to the RNC, 15,000 bucks to the RNC.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:07.230
And what you have to understand is this, it
might seem like there's a contradiction here.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:16.580
How can MSNBC be both quote, leftist editorially
in their content and be financially supporting

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.910
the reelection of of Donald Trump?

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:23.480
There's actually a lot of cynicism that is
required here in order to understand this.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:27.920
First of all, remember that a lot of these
big corporations are donating to both sides

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.200
because they want to be able to Curry favor
with whoever it is that is ultimately going

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.200
to be an office.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.670
And the truth is that on a lot of issues,
including regulatory issues with media, you've

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.140
got similarities between Republicans and Democrats.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:45.760
On net neutrality, you've seen a significant
division between Democrats and Republicans,

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.510
but on a lot of other issues that impact the
day to day operation of these big media conglomerates,

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.520
Republicans and Democrats are both willing
to help, let's put it that way.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.750
The second thing to understand is that MSNBC
is not progressive.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:08.030
It's an establishment democratic editorial
perspective shrouded in veiling, I guess veiling,

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.030
I guess we would say.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:13.450
Um, a, a big corporate priorities that are
very similar.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.659
When you look at Fox as news corporation,
parent company and MSNBC is Comcast.

00:04:18.659 --> 00:04:26.550
They are liberal, center left really editorially
as a business decision, but a lot of their

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.909
priorities are very, very similar to any other
media conglomerate.

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:34.499
Now, number three, Trump being president is
clearly good for MSNBC.

00:04:34.499 --> 00:04:35.669
His ratings.

00:04:35.669 --> 00:04:38.219
I've admitted it's good for our show as well.

00:04:38.219 --> 00:04:42.520
I've not hidden from you that our show has
had some of the most explosive growth in our

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.249
history under Donald Trump's presidency.

00:04:45.249 --> 00:04:46.669
I don't want the guy reelected.

00:04:46.669 --> 00:04:47.930
I don't plan to vote for him.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.340
I want what's best for the country.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.349
But I've admitted that because it is part
of the interesting thing that has been taking

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:56.979
place in this country over the last several
years.

00:04:56.979 --> 00:05:03.439
But if it has to be a Democrat, MSNBC wants
it to be a centrist establishment Democrat.

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:07.139
Now, as far as the voters point, she's right.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:12.050
Um, some of the analyses on MSNBC have been
horrible.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:15.809
Chuck Todd talking about Bernie supporters
as Brown shirts.

00:05:15.809 --> 00:05:22.779
Absolutely crazy comparing raw support for
Bernie in 20 when there were two serious candidates

00:05:22.779 --> 00:05:28.370
with support for Bernie in 2020 when there
are five at least serious candidates.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.900
It's not just cynical, it's dishonest.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.279
It's deceptive.

00:05:32.279 --> 00:05:33.629
And kudos to Ari Malborough.

00:05:33.629 --> 00:05:36.039
I've always seen Ari as a pretty good faith
actor.

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:41.339
He seems genuinely wanting to know how the
world is rather than to sort of push a particular

00:05:41.339 --> 00:05:42.339
worldview.

00:05:42.339 --> 00:05:44.439
He seemed genuinely interested in learning
more.

00:05:44.439 --> 00:05:49.080
It might've been more interesting if the person
doing the interviews from for MSNBC had been

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:53.379
more caught off guard in some way or reacted
in some way, but it was already Melbourne

00:05:53.379 --> 00:05:56.550
doing the interviews and he seemed genuinely
interested in hearing what this voter had

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.550
to say.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:03.300
Uh, I'm curious whether other folks have experienced
a similar push where the coverage of a particular

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:08.650
candidate by a particular network has pushed
them towards or away candidates.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:07:06.559
Let me know.

